    "id": "guide_fac_comp_survival",
    "title": "Faculty of Computing Freshers Survival Guide (What Tech School Is Really Like)",
    "summary": "Insider blueprint for freshers in Computer Science, AI, Cybersecurity, IT, and Software Engineering on balancing coding labs, math rigors, and practical tech skills.",
    "content": "Computing Freshers Survival Guide\n\n(What Coding School Is Really Like)\n\nWelcome\n\nThis book is produced by the Disciple Christian Network to help you navigate university life wisely and intentionally.\n\nIt is not theory.\n\nIt is built from real conversations with senior students who have walked this path, made mistakes, paid prices, and learned what truly works on campus.\n\nMany of them said one thing: “I wish I knew this in my first year.”\n\nThink of this book as a starter guide for your first year—your academics, your decisions, your time, and your mindset. This book is also part of something bigger.\n\nThe FRESHERS’ AWAKENING Conference is coming—a moment created to open your eyes early, reset your mindset, and help you start school the right way.\n\nBecause the truth is simple:\n\nWhat you do in your first year matters more than you think.\n\nThrough this book and our community, we are here to help you:\n\nAvoid common freshman mistakes\n\nBuild strong academic habits early\n\nMake better decisions on campus\n\nChoose excellence over regret\n\nTo stay connected and get updates, resources, and opportunities, join our WhatsApp communities:\n\n🔹 https://chat.whatsapp.com/FmBJkhA6fui9CKhcmhGgip\n\n🔹 https://chat.whatsapp.com/ExJNGyUxPJu0581Nn9P8aK\n\nWelcome to school.\n\nMore importantly, welcome to doing it right.\n\n\n## THE COMP-SCI COMPASS\n\n**A Strategic Guide to Excelling in Computer Science at the University of Ibadan**\n\n\n### Chapter 1: The Threshold of Greatness\n\nMy dearest Fresher, take a deep breath and relax. **You have made it.**\n\nGaining admission into the University of Ibadan, particularly the Department of Computer Science, is a massive achievement. It is perfectly normal to feel a mix of excitement, nerves, and perhaps a bit of intimidation by UI’s reputation. We all felt that way when we first stepped into these halls.\n\nThis guide isn't here to scare you; it’s here to arm you. Computer Science is demanding, but a First Class—or even a perfect CGPA—is very achievable with the right mindset.\n\n\n#### What is Computer Science, Really?\n\nIt’s more than just “coding.” It is the art of problem-solving and logical thinking. You will soon be diving into:\n\n**Algorithms:** The logic behind every digital action.\n\n**Mathematics & Statistics:** The true languages of computing.\n\n**Systems & Data:** Understanding how software breathes and interacts.\n\n**Broad Horizons:** You'll even touch on English and French to ensure you are a well-rounded graduate.\n\n\n### Chapter 2: The Freedom Trap\n\nUniversity learning is a complete departure from secondary school. In this environment, you are given total freedom, but that freedom requires **total self-discipline.**\n\n**The Reality Check:**\n\nNo one will chase you to attend 8:00 AM lectures.\n\nNo one will remind you that a deadline is approaching.\n\nNo one will force you to open your books.\n\nMany students struggle not because they lack intelligence, but because they underestimate the responsibility that comes with freedom. To stay ahead, you must learn to research independently, Google effectively, and practice coding rather than just copying it.\n\n\n### Chapter 3: Mastering the Curriculum\n\nDifficulty is relative. A course is \"easy\" if you know it and \"difficult\" if you don't. Approach every unit with a **conqueror’s mentality.** The \"Big Five\" Courses to Watch\n\n**COS 101:** Do not rely solely on slides. Read textbooks (like Solomani), ask questions immediately when you’re lost, and treat your project with the utmost diligence—it’s a heavy grade-lifter.\n\n**MTH 102:** Often feared as the \"fresher’s nightmare,\" yet students have scored 98/100 in it. Fear is the only thing that will actually stop you from learning. Start early and solve the \"difficult\" problems first.\n\n**PHY 102:** This course punishes latecomers. If you wait until the end of the semester to start reading, it will be an uphill battle. Revise weekly.\n\n**COS 102 & 103:** If you don’t have a background in **Python**, start learning the basics *now*. These courses form your foundation; don’t build on a shaky base.\n\n\n### Chapter 4: Deciphering the Lecturers\n\nYou’ll hear many rumors—some say UI lecturers are sadists, others say they are angels. The truth lies in the Golden Rule: *“Do unto others as you would have them do unto you.”*\n\n**How to Build Respectful Academic Relationships:**\n\n**Professionalism:** This includes your speech, your actions, and your dressing. Decency opens doors.\n\n**Effort First:** Don't ask for help until you’ve attempted the work yourself. Lecturers respect students who show \"skin in the game.\"\n\n**Visibility:** Attend tutorials and consultations. They notice effort even when they seem strict.\n\n\n### Chapter 5: Your Circle and Your Choices\n\nThe Bible says, *\"Show me your friend and I will show you who you are.\"* Your social circle is the greatest predictor of your CGPA.\n\n**Choose friends who:**\n\nBelieve and trust in God.\n\nAre consistently present in the front rows of the lecture hall.\n\nCan discuss academics without feeling \"un-cool.\"\n\nEncourage growth rather than constant \"enjoyment\" and distraction.\n\n\n#### Common Freshman Pitfalls to Avoid:\n\nBelieving 100-level results don’t matter (They are the foundation of your CGPA).\n\nSkipping lectures because \"the slides are online.\"\n\nCopying assignments without understanding the *why* behind the answer.\n\n\n### Chapter 6: The Balanced Life\n\nBeing a \"rounded\" student means looking beyond the four walls of the classroom.\n\n**Time & Money:** Create a personal timetable and stick to it. Budget your allowance—unnecessary spending leads to unnecessary stress.\n\n**Mental Health:** If you are struggling emotionally, you aren't failing. Talk to seniors or trusted friends. UI is tough; support is allowed.\n\n**Beyond the Grades:** Join tech clubs, attend bootcamps, and volunteer for leadership roles. University is about growth and exposure as much as it is about certificates.\n\n\n### Final Word\n\nBe consistent. Be disciplined. Think long-term.\n\nYour time at UI will pass faster than you expect. What you build now—academically, mentally, and spiritually—will follow you for the rest of your life.\n\nGo forth and conquer.\n\n**DAVID, Chairman Obadolowo***, 2024/2025 Set* *Department of Computer Science, University of Ibadan.*\n\n\n### DCN HAS PREPARED AN EXAM SUCCESS BLUEPRINT JUST FOR YOU.\n\nExam Success Blueprint Part 1 — https://t.me/dcnmedia/1982\n\nExam Success Blueprint Part 2 — https://t.me/dcnmedia/1983\n\nExam Success Blueprint Part 3 — https://t.me/dcnmedia/1984\n\n\n### DCN HAS ALSO PREPARED A WAY TO BE THE BEST STUDENT IN YOUR CLASS.\n\nThe God Kind Of Result Part 1 — https://t.me/dcnmedia/1584\n\nThe God Kind Of Result Part 2 — https://t.me/dcnmedia/1585\n\nThe God Kind Of Result Part 3 — https://t.me/dcnmedia/1586\n\nAll Round Success — https://t.me/dcnmedia/1341\n\nExcelling By God — https://t.me/dcnmedia/2055\n\nHow God Helps Students — https://t.me/dcnmedia/1717\n\nResults-Oriented Learning — https://t.me/dcnmedia/1716\n\nBalance As A Christian Student — https://t.me/dcnmedia/167\n\nThe Law Of Diligence — https://t.me/dcnmedia/1942\n\n\n### DCN HAS ALSO PREPARED SOMETHING THAT WILL MAKE YOU A GIANT.\n\nPictures Of Greatness Part 1 — https://t.me/dcnmedia/1980\n\nPictures Of Greatness Part 2 — https://t.me/dcnmedia/1981",
